    <section class="report-section">

        <h2>SmartX IoT Dashboard: Engagement and Interactivity Strategies</h2>

        <p>
            IoT dashboards must do more than display raw sensor readings. They should help users
            notice abnormal behaviour, understand what is happening and decide what action to take.
            Research in visual analytics shows that anomaly information can be emphasised to direct
            attention to important sensor events [1], interactive analysis can support diagnosis [2],
            and explanatory feedback can help users investigate the physical cause of an anomaly [3].
            Five strategies were therefore considered for SmartX.
        </p>

    </section>

    <section class="report-section">

        <h2>Five Engagement and Interactivity Strategies</h2>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">1</div>

            <div>
                <h3>Severity-Based Anomaly Highlighting</h3>

                <p>
                    Device conditions can be classified as Healthy, Warning or Critical so that abnormal
                    states receive greater visual emphasis. Janetzko et al. [1] show that highlighting
                    anomaly information helps analysts focus on important parts of large sensor datasets.
                    In SmartX this can quickly draw attention to low soil moisture, high power use or
                    missing telemetry.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">2</div>

            <div>
                <h3>Interactive Device Inspection and Drill-Down</h3>

                <p>
                    A high-level dashboard should allow the operator to inspect an individual device when
                    more detail is required. Xu et al. [2] demonstrate that interactive visual analytics
                    can support both detection and diagnosis. SmartX therefore presents device-specific
                    details such as name, MAC address, deployment location, latest metric and health status.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">3</div>

            <div>
                <h3>Current Telemetry and Status Visualisation</h3>

                <p>
                    Showing the latest meaningful reading helps the user understand why a device has a
                    particular status. Sensor values such as soil moisture, temperature, power consumption
                    and valve state can be interpreted more easily when displayed together with the device
                    condition [1].
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">4</div>

            <div>
                <h3>Context-Aware Explanations and Guided Troubleshooting</h3>

                <p>
                    A warning becomes more useful when the system also explains the likely reason and
                    recommends a next step. Patabendige et al. [3] used anomaly explanations to help users
                    investigate unusual smart-water behaviour. SmartX applies the same principle by
                    providing guidance such as checking irrigation, sensor placement, device power or
                    network connectivity.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="strategy-card">
            <div class="strategy-number">5</div>

            <div>
                <h3>Action-Oriented Interaction</h3>

                <p>
                    Engagement should lead to useful action rather than only visual appeal. SmartX connects
                    device registration, telemetry ingestion, health classification and troubleshooting
                    guidance. This allows a user to submit data, observe the system response and decide what
                    to investigate next, supporting the human-in-the-loop approach described in interactive
                    anomaly analysis [2], [3].
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

    </section>

    <section class="report-section selected-strategy">

        <h2>Selected Strategy for SmartX</h2>

        <div class="selected-title">
            Severity-Based Contextual Anomaly Highlighting with Guided Troubleshooting
        </div>

        <p>
            The primary engagement strategy selected for SmartX is severity-based contextual anomaly
            highlighting combined with guided troubleshooting. This strategy was chosen because SmartX
            is intended for technical monitoring of connected sensors, where the most important task is
            not simply displaying data but helping an operator recognise abnormal conditions and decide
            what should happen next.
        </p>

        <p>
            IoT systems can generate many readings from environmental sensors, power meters and actuators.
            If every value is presented with equal visual importance, the operator must continually inspect
            each reading and decide manually whether it is normal. This increases cognitive effort and makes
            important events easier to miss. Janetzko et al. [1] demonstrate how anomaly information can be
            visually emphasised to guide analysts toward meaningful areas of sensor data. SmartX applies this
            idea at device level by using Healthy, Warning and Critical states. Normal devices can remain
            visually unobtrusive while abnormal devices immediately receive attention.
        </p>

        <p>
            Highlighting alone is not enough. A technical user also needs to understand why the device has
            entered an abnormal state. Xu et al. [2] show the value of interactive visual analytics for both
            detecting and diagnosing unusual behaviour. SmartX therefore presents contextual information
            alongside the status, including the device name, location, latest metric, latest reading and last
            contact time. This allows the operator to connect the classification to a real device and an
            observable condition instead of treating the alert as an isolated message.
        </p>

        <p>
            The final part of the strategy is guided troubleshooting. Patabendige et al. [3] demonstrate the
            value of providing a rationale that helps users investigate the physical cause of an anomaly.
            SmartX follows this principle by pairing its health classification with a recommendation. A
            critically low soil-moisture reading can prompt the user to inspect irrigation or sensor placement,
            while a telemetry timeout can recommend checking power, network connectivity and gateway
            registration. The interface therefore supports the next practical action rather than stopping at
            detection.
        </p>

        <p>
            This strategy also makes the dashboard more engaging because it reacts visibly to incoming
            telemetry. When a reading changes, the device state and the recommended response can change as
            well. This creates a direct relationship between data ingestion, system analysis and user action.
            The chosen strategy therefore supports three essential activities: awareness, interpretation and
            response. Severity indicators create awareness, contextual telemetry supports interpretation, and
            troubleshooting guidance helps the operator respond appropriately.
        </p>

    </section>

    <section class="report-section">

        <h2>Application in SmartX</h2>

        <p>
            SmartX implements the strategy across the Sensor Data and Device Health sections. A user registers
            a device and submits double, integer or Boolean telemetry through the ingestion gateway. The health
            logic then evaluates the latest reading and can classify the device as Healthy, Warning or Critical.
            Low soil moisture, excessive power consumption and communication timeouts can therefore produce
            different states and recommendations.
        </p>

