    <section class="report-section">

        <h2>Conclusion</h2>

        <p>
            The five strategies considered for SmartX were severity-based highlighting, device drill-down,
            current telemetry visualisation, guided troubleshooting and action-oriented interaction. Research
            supports anomaly emphasis [1], interactive diagnosis [2] and explanatory feedback [3]. SmartX
            therefore uses severity-based contextual anomaly highlighting with guided troubleshooting to turn
            raw telemetry into meaningful device states and practical user guidance.
        </p>

    </section>


    <section class="report-section">
        <h2>Part 3 Extension: Network Topology and Mesh Routing</h2>
        <p>
            The original Task 1 research above focuses on engagement, anomaly highlighting and guided
            troubleshooting. The final POE extends the SmartX application with a separate network
            topology module. This section documents the data structures and algorithms implemented
            for Part 3; it does not change the original Task 1 research or its reference list.
        </p>
        <h3>AVL Tree for Historical Telemetry</h3>
        <p>
            Processed stream readings are indexed by timestamp in an AVL tree. Each node stores
            its timestamp, readings with that timestamp, child references and height. Insertions
            rebalance the tree using rotations. An inclusive date-range query visits readings
            chronologically and can optionally filter by sensor ID. The AVL index supplements
            the existing Part 2 sorted history rather than replacing it.
        </p>
        <h3>Weighted Graph for the Mesh</h3>
        <p>
            Registered SmartX devices become vertices in an undirected graph. An operator can add
            weighted links to represent simulated wireless connections. Each link is stored in
            both directions. The network diagram shows these links and identifies simulated offline
            devices. Link weights are user-entered costs, not live radio measurements.
        </p>
        <h3>Breadth-First Search (BFS)</h3>
        <p>
            BFS explores neighbouring devices level by level. It determines which nodes can be
            reached from a source and reconstructs a route with the fewest hops to a destination.
            Offline nodes are excluded. Because BFS minimises hop count, the route is not
            necessarily the route with the lowest total link weight.
        </p>
        <h3>Prim's Minimum Spanning Tree</h3>
        <p>
            Prim's algorithm selects low-weight links that connect reachable vertices without
            cycles. The dashboard highlights selected links in blue and displays their combined
            weight. If the graph is disconnected, the implementation returns a minimum spanning
            forest: one tree for each connected component. Offline nodes are excluded from
            this calculation.
        </p>
        <h3>Simulated Failure and Recovery</h3>
        <p>
            Operators can temporarily mark a device offline and restore it through the mesh
            dashboard. Route discovery and the minimum spanning forest then exclude offline
            devices. These are application-level simulations, not physical ESP32 failures or
            automatic wireless network discovery.
        </p>
        <h3>Technology Considerations</h3>
        <p>
            The current ASP.NET Core API and Blazor WebAssembly client support a transparent,
            testable simulation without requiring physical hardware. For a future deployment,
            persistent storage would prevent loss of topology and telemetry after API restarts;
            authenticated device management would restrict failure and link changes; and
            measured signal strength or latency could supply meaningful link weights.
            Live updates would also require an event or push mechanism rather than manual refresh.
            These are recommendations for future work, not implemented features.
        </p>
    </section>

